WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.562 --> 00:00:06.020
MINE CETINKAYA-RUNDEL: Hi, my
name is Mine Cetinkaya-Rundel

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.450
and I am a Developer
Educator at Posit,

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:12.110
as well as a Professor of the
Practice at Duke University.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:14.840
And in this video, I'd like
to introduce you to Quarto.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.910
So if you've been hearing
a lot about Quarto

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:19.850
and wanting to dive in
and have been waiting

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:21.770
to see when is the
right time for me,

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:23.570
or if you've played
with it a little bit

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:26.810
and want to get a sense
of what more I can do

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:28.820
and what more you
can do with it,

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:31.380
hopefully, this will be a
good introduction for you.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.760
So we are going to start
things pretty simply.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:38.840
We're going to start with
a single Quarto document.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.140
I am going to open
that up in RStudio.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:43.970
So let's go ahead
and open that up.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:47.600
My Quarto document
is called index.qmd.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.480
For those of you who are
familiar with R Markdown,

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.220
you're probably familiar with
file suffixes called rmd.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.010
And Quarto documents
are called qmd.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.140
At the top of this
document, things

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:01.560
should look pretty familiar.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.840
We have a YAML with some
metadata about our document--

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.990
a title, the format that
we want to output to.

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.570
So we're going to make
an HTML document first.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:14.960
And I'm going to use the
RStudio visual editor.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.080
So again, if you are
familiar with R Markdown

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:21.980
and haven't played with
RStudio visual editor lately,

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.020
you can switch over
to the source editor,

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:27.090
and things should look
pretty familiar to you.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:31.520
But let's go back to the visual
editor where you get a document

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.490
that feels a bit more like
Google Docs or writing

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:38.360
in an app-like notion where
things are a little bit more

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:39.860
what-you-see-is-what-you-get.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:43.370
For example, I have some
information about the data set

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:46.070
I'm going to use here--
the Penguins data set from

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:47.810
the palmerpenguins package--

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:50.840
and I might want to bold
the name of the package.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:54.090
And I can do that by
using the toolbar here

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:59.040
or I can use keyboard
shortcuts to do that as well.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.150
The visual editor has other
handy tools on the toolbar.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:06.150
For example, I can directly
add a link to this.

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:10.570
So let's go ahead and add a link
to the palmerpenguins website.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:15.310
And hit OK.

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:17.750
So that now is a live link.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:21.760
And if we were to switch over
back to the source editor,

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.700
if you are familiar with
writing things in Markdown,

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.530
you can see that
under the hood, it is

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:28.353
writing the Markdown for you.

00:02:28.353 --> 00:02:29.770
It's just that you
are not the one

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:31.420
having to write the Markdown.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:34.630
So let's go ahead and
render this document

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:36.715
and let's see what
it looks like.

00:02:36.715 --> 00:02:41.080
A setting that I like
to use on my RStudio IDE

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:47.110
is I generally like to preview
my documents in the viewer pane

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:50.830
so that I can see my source
code on one side and my render

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:52.660
document on the other side.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.900
So we should be able to see
our render document here

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.080
in the viewer pane on the
right-hand side of our RStudio

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:00.760
IDE.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.670
Things will look pretty similar
between the source document

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:09.220
and the render document because
our source document is also

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:11.620
using this visual format.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:14.380
But the difference is
that on the source side,

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:18.490
we only have the code,
and on the output side,

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:22.660
we're seeing the code and the
output to go along with it.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:26.740
So let's introduce you to a
few of Quarto's features now.

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:31.690
We can see that we are taking a
look at the palmerpenguins data

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:34.840
set, and we have our
visualizations here for it.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:37.850
But we might want to take a
look at the actual data itself.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:41.900
So I am going to go ahead
and insert a code chunk here,

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:47.560
which I can do either with the
Insert, Code Chunk menu here

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:51.410
or using my keyboard shortcut
with the forward slash

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:55.300
and bringing up the
Insert Anything toolbar.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:58.500
And I'll say I want
an R Code Chunk.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.500
And let's say that I want to
glimpse at the penguins data

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:04.600
set.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.580
And let's go ahead and
Render that document

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:10.900
and we'll be able to see
the input and the output

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:13.330
on the viewer side.

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:15.910
Scrolling down on
this document, I

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:19.660
have a code chunk
that generates a plot.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:22.450
So one thing that
I might want to do

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:27.280
is add an alternative text
to this figure, for example.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:32.050
And I can do that by using
the fig-alt chunk option.

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:36.110
The visual editor nicely
does YAML completion for me

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:39.730
so I can choose from here which
chunk option I want to use.

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:41.470
I'm going to go with fig-alt.

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:44.960
And I have a
multi-line chunk option

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:47.290
so I'm going to use
this vertical bar here.

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:48.850
I've already
written the alt text

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:51.340
so I will just copy and
paste that here so you don't

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:53.218
have to watch me type this out.

00:04:53.218 --> 00:04:54.760
But basically, we're
saying that this

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.270
is a scatterplot of penguins'
bill depth and length colored

00:04:58.270 --> 00:04:59.350
by species.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:01.660
And we say something
about the trend we see.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:04.870
It's relatively strong,
and a linear association.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:07.090
Let's go ahead and
Render this document.

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:09.910
And when we Render the
document, the alternative text

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:12.760
won't show on the output,
because again, it's

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:14.800
not part of the
output necessarily

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.840
but the underlying source code.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:21.130
But if I pop this out
to a browser window

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:25.600
and scroll down to my
figure and right-click on it

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:28.610
to inspect it

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:32.870
I should be able to see that
the alt text is actually

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:35.900
available for me under the hood.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:39.860
If I'm writing a report, I might
want to not necessarily show

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:41.910
all of this code on the output.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:46.700
So one thing I can do is
I can hide the code one

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:53.360
chunk at a time with another
chunk option, echo: false,

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:56.660
and that will only hide
that particular chunk

00:05:56.660 --> 00:06:00.300
option, that particular
code chunk's code.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.970
But if I want to do that
at the document level,

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:04.890
I have a few options as well.

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:09.810
I can actually go to the
YAML on top of my document.

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:15.800
And under execute, I can say
I want to do echo: false.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:18.720
And let's go ahead and
Render this full document.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:22.070
And we will see that all
of the code is now hidden

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:23.870
and we only have
the output, which

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:27.390
might be preferable for you
depending on your audience,

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:29.540
whether you want them
to see the code or not.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:32.990
And here we go.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:36.260
Here is our output
without any of the code.

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:38.900
Over here, we have
a figure that's

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:40.640
being generated with code.

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:44.390
And in the text, I have said
the figure below is a bar--

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:46.460
is actually a scatterplot.

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:47.480
Let's go ahead and fix that

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:51.620
That scatterplot of
species of penguins.

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:53.850
We can add
cross-references to this.

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:55.910
So, in order to add
a cross-reference,

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:57.510
I need to do two things.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:00.320
First, I need to
change my chunk label

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:04.620
to include the word fig at
the beginning as a suffix.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:06.230
So that's fig dash.

00:07:06.230 --> 00:07:10.770
And second, I need
to add a caption.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:14.840
So to add a caption, I
will use the fig-cap.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:20.580
And maybe let's go ahead
and re-use this information

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:21.080
from here.

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:32.250
All right, so now I
have a figure that has--

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:35.640
a label that starts
with fig and a caption.

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:37.380
All right, so let's
highlight this,

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:42.730
and let's say we want to
Insert a Cross Reference.

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:45.810
The figure is available
for me to choose here.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:48.520
And if I Render the
document one more time,

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:51.340
it will create a link
to the figure itself.

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:53.010
So now I don't need
to worry about when

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:56.310
I render my document, if it's
below or above, particularly

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:58.800
if I'm outputting to
something like PDF.

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:01.960
I can do the same thing
with tables as well.

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:04.200
The only difference
is that I would

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:08.010
need to add the tbl
for table suffix.

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:13.380
And similarly, I need to
add a caption for that too.

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:18.100
So this will be, let's
say, the first 10 penguins.

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:25.750
And let's go ahead and do this
without using the Insert menu.

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:28.840
I can simply grab
the label and I

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:31.600
can say-- instead
of the table below,

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:33.789
at and the name of my table.

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:35.590
And I can Render my document.

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:40.210
And it will do the counting
and the labeling of my figures

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:41.210
and tables for me.

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:44.049
So I have a figure
1 here and then

00:08:44.049 --> 00:08:46.780
a table that's going to be
cross-referenced underneath it

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:47.290
as well.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:52.300
And voila, here we go.

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:54.030
We also have a Table 1.

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:56.790
The last thing I'd like to
show in the visual editor

00:08:56.790 --> 00:09:00.610
is adding citations, which can
be a huge hassle sometimes.

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:04.780
So here we are going to cite
where these data come from.

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:06.690
So in order to do
that, I'm going

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:09.610
to say I want to
Insert a Citation.

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:12.780
And you can see that I have some
options to choose from in terms

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:15.480
of where my citations
can come, but I

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:19.980
am going to go ahead and
grab the DOI of the paper

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:24.960
where the palmerpenguins data
were originally published.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:29.410
And I can say that I
want to Insert this.

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:32.670
And when I do so,
two things happen.

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:35.550
Reference gets added here.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:39.360
And you can see that my
YAML already also gained

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.820
a new line called bibliography.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:44.310
And if I go to my
files, I have actually

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:47.040
gained that file
called references.bib.

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:52.660
And in this file is the bib
reference for this paper.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:56.970
So if we go ahead and
Render this document,

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:59.950
we will see that the
reference has been added.

00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:02.040
If I hover over
it, I can see it.

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:03.990
Or if I click on
it, it will take me

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:07.080
to the end of my document
where I have a section called

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:09.490
References inserted for me.

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:14.130
So we've talked about creating
a single document with Quarto.

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:17.310
And this has been a
HTML document so far.

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:21.510
If I want to change the output
to something like a PDF,

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:24.570
all I need to do is
indicate that on my YAML.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:27.195
So let's go ahead and
give that a Render too.

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:31.880
I don't need to change
anything in terms

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:34.680
of the syntax in my document.

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:35.180
Right.

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:38.870
Now we have a PDF document
with the same content,

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:41.060
also hiding the code.

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:45.420
So respecting the YAML options
that we had indicated earlier.

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:47.960
And the nice thing
about this PDF document

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:52.280
is not only was I able
to obtain it so easily,

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:55.010
but it also shows up in
my Viewer tab as opposed

00:10:55.010 --> 00:10:57.410
to a pop-up window.

00:10:57.410 --> 00:11:00.080
Now let's go ahead and
change things up even more.

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:02.450
We've gone from a
single HTML document

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:04.430
to a single PDF document.

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:06.050
What about making slides?

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:09.140
So I'm going to make
HTML-based slides using

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:11.730
our revealjs as my output.

00:11:11.730 --> 00:11:15.020
And once again, I am not
going to touch anything

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:16.040
in my document.

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:18.410
The only thing
RStudio asks me to do

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:20.990
is to reload the
visual editor when

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:23.150
I change the output format.

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:26.190
And let's go ahead and
Render that as well.

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:27.230
And here we go.

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:30.860
Our document title
is now a title slide.

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:34.220
I can actually scroll
through the different slides

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:35.270
that I have.

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:39.710
Each of them are determined
by these headings.

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:43.130
So let's go ahead and
actually turn this

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:46.160
into a slightly more
reasonable slide deck now.

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:49.970
Things worked but my text
is a little bit squished.

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:56.240
I am going to take a
look at the Outline view

00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:58.970
here, where I can see
the different sections

00:11:58.970 --> 00:11:59.770
in my document.

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:08.090
And I'm going to make these
second-level headings.

00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:11.800
So these particular Data,
Species, and Penguins

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:13.900
are going to be
second-level headings.

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:16.600
And let's go ahead and insert
some first-level headings

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:19.600
as well using the
Insert Anything tool.

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:22.180
We'll say that I want
a first-level heading.

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:26.110
Maybe first, I want
something like Introduction.

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:35.560
And then at the end, I might
want something like Analysis.

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:38.410
And maybe a slide
here, something

00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:40.430
like Modeling results.

00:12:43.390 --> 00:12:47.230
And then I might finally
want another slide

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:49.540
at the end called References.

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:51.100
Remember those
references that I had,

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:53.630
I might want to move
them to the very end.

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:56.440
So I've created this
hierarchical structure

00:12:56.440 --> 00:12:58.690
for my document
that I can nicely

00:12:58.690 --> 00:13:00.770
view here in the outline.

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:02.770
And if I Render my
document, I should

00:13:02.770 --> 00:13:08.530
be able to see that that same
outline is apparent in my slide

00:13:08.530 --> 00:13:09.430
deck as well.

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:11.500
My first-level
headings are going

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:15.310
to turn into section
dividers in my slides.

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:17.440
And then my
second-level headings

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:21.470
are going to turn into
indicators for a new slide.

00:13:21.470 --> 00:13:23.930
So we can see that
same outline here.

00:13:23.930 --> 00:13:27.340
And I can go ahead and
scroll through my slides.

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:30.850
Some things that I'm seeing
here, for this data slide,

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:32.590
things look a little too big.

00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:34.580
Maybe things are not filling in.

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:38.110
So using the visual editor,
I can start tweaking things.

00:13:38.110 --> 00:13:41.320
I can write full-on
CSS for my slides,

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:44.480
but I can also use some
of the predefined classes.

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:48.920
So one of my favorite is the
smaller class, for example.

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:52.480
So let's go ahead and
do that and Render that.

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:56.050
And that should squish my text,
make it a little bit smaller,

00:13:56.050 --> 00:13:59.200
allowing me to fit a bit
more text into my slides.

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:01.510
Never a great idea to
put a lot of text on your

00:14:01.510 --> 00:14:03.760
slides, but if you're
in a pinch and you just

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:06.970
want things to be a little bit
smaller so everything fits,

00:14:06.970 --> 00:14:09.820
this is a really nice fix.

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:15.550
Let's also go ahead and
get rid of this option.

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:18.220
Or actually, let's go
ahead and say that I

00:14:18.220 --> 00:14:21.910
do want the code to be shown.

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:23.780
So I'm going to say echo: true.

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:25.570
So let's say I'm
teaching, and I actually

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:28.010
want to show the code as well.

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:30.010
We'll see that our
slides get busy

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:32.920
again because we have both
the code and the output

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:34.210
on one slide.

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:37.090
This one looks pretty
fine, but over here, we're

00:14:37.090 --> 00:14:41.740
seeing that the figure is
squished in order to make room

00:14:41.740 --> 00:14:43.210
for the code.

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:46.070
Well, we have a remedy
for that as well.

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:50.380
What I'm going to say is
for this particular slide

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:53.890
and this code chunk, I
want the output location

00:14:53.890 --> 00:14:57.280
to be, let's say,
column-fragment

00:14:57.280 --> 00:15:00.100
and see what that gets us.

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:02.980
So what this is going
to do is put my code

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:07.690
in one column of my slides and
my output in the other column

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:08.830
of my slides.

00:15:08.830 --> 00:15:13.660
But it will actually add some
sort of animation to my slides

00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:15.320
and delay the output.

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:17.050
So I might get
something like this

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:20.530
where when I move to the next
slide, I can see the code.

00:15:20.530 --> 00:15:22.390
This still looks
pretty squished so

00:15:22.390 --> 00:15:25.780
if I don't want that, I can
choose a different option once

00:15:25.780 --> 00:15:28.720
again using the YAML
completion with my tab.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:32.120
And I might say, OK, just move
it to the next slide for me.

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:33.715
So the plot moves
to the next slide.

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:41.310
So we've made a single
document so far.

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:48.240
And we have made
also a slide deck.

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:50.490
Now let's imagine
a situation where

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:52.710
you have multiple
documents, a project,

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:54.930
and you want to put them
all together in something

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:56.430
like a website.

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:58.860
So what we're going to do is--

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:05.430
let's go back and change
the format to HTML again.

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:11.410
Reload our visual editor
and Render this document

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:14.650
as a single HTML document.

00:16:14.650 --> 00:16:18.130
And let's go ahead and
add one more document,

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:25.880
maybe something
that says "about,"

00:16:25.880 --> 00:16:28.010
something like an About page.

00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:35.120
For our web page, I'm going to
create a new document called

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:41.030
About, probably has some
boilerplate, our language here.

00:16:41.030 --> 00:16:44.160
Maybe let's go ahead
and select all of that

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:48.465
and delete it and say,
this is a demo website.

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:57.140
And I'm going to save
this file as about.qmd.

00:17:01.910 --> 00:17:05.420
And now I have two
documents, two qmd documents

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:06.200
in my project.

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:10.069
An about.qmd and an index.qmd.

00:17:10.069 --> 00:17:12.210
And I have their
outputs as well.

00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:13.829
Let's go ahead and
clear those out,

00:17:13.829 --> 00:17:18.890
those outputs so that I have
just my source files here.

00:17:18.890 --> 00:17:21.770
To bring these two together
in a website format,

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:29.300
I just need one file,
plain text file,

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:34.670
specifically called underscore
quarto dot YAML, dot Y-M-L.

00:17:34.670 --> 00:17:38.480
And in here, I need to
actually tell Quarto

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:40.560
how to bring things together.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:43.580
So I want a Quarto project.

00:17:43.580 --> 00:17:46.940
I want the type of this
project to be website.

00:17:46.940 --> 00:17:48.740
Note that I'm not
even typing these.

00:17:48.740 --> 00:17:51.170
I'm using the YAML completion.

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:55.220
And in this website,
I want the title

00:17:55.220 --> 00:18:03.710
of my website to be
perhaps, welcome to Quarto.

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:07.010
And I want a navbar.

00:18:07.010 --> 00:18:11.480
Let's say that on
the left, I would

00:18:11.480 --> 00:18:18.140
like to link to my index.qmd
file and my about.qmd file.

00:18:21.650 --> 00:18:26.510
And let's go ahead
and save this.

00:18:26.510 --> 00:18:32.240
And I am going to just relaunch
this project from the project

00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:34.790
navigator in RStudio.

00:18:34.790 --> 00:18:37.350
And once I relaunch
this project,

00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:40.580
we can see that I gained
a new tab called Build.

00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:49.300
And let's go ahead
in this new Build

00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:52.480
tab, click on Render Website.

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:54.670
And voila, I have a website.

00:18:54.670 --> 00:18:56.710
Let's pop this out
to a browser window

00:18:56.710 --> 00:18:59.590
so we can see things a
little bit more nicely.

00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:04.120
I can see that on top, I have
a navigation bar with my two

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:05.860
pages, my Hello, Penguins!

00:19:05.860 --> 00:19:10.810
page with my analysis
and also my About page.

00:19:10.810 --> 00:19:12.940
And they're automatically
linked to that.

00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:15.820
And basically, clicking
on the name of the website

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:19.370
takes me back to my home page.

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:22.000
Let me show you one
other feature that's

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:26.800
actually a newer feature of
Quarto, starting with 1.3.

00:19:26.800 --> 00:19:31.360
Let's go ahead, and for this
index page, add another format.

00:19:31.360 --> 00:19:37.720
So I am going to say that I
do want an HTML with default

00:19:37.720 --> 00:19:44.302
options, but I also want a PDF
of the same document as well.

00:19:44.302 --> 00:19:46.635
And let's go ahead and render
the website one more time.

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:51.500
And if I take a look
at my output now,

00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:54.290
we can see that not
only do I have the HTML

00:19:54.290 --> 00:19:58.640
file on my website, but I have
an ability to actually download

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:00.870
a PDF version of my document.

00:20:00.870 --> 00:20:06.080
So if you are creating websites
but you have artifacts in there

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:09.860
that you would like people to
be able to download as PDFs,

00:20:09.860 --> 00:20:13.260
I think this is a really
handy tool for doing so.

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:17.240
So we've gone from a single
document to a slide deck,

00:20:17.240 --> 00:20:20.840
to a website, but all of this
is living on my computer.

00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:23.300
To wrap things up,
let's talk about how

00:20:23.300 --> 00:20:26.150
do I get this and make
it available to others.

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:30.860
Obviously, I could use version
control to get things to GitHub

00:20:30.860 --> 00:20:33.560
and publish from there,
but another option

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:35.420
is to use Quarto Pub.

00:20:35.420 --> 00:20:40.070
So I am going to now
go to my Terminal.

00:20:40.070 --> 00:20:48.105
And in my terminal, I am going
to type publish or quarto

00:20:48.105 --> 00:20:48.605
publish.

00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:54.230
And when I hit
quarto publish, it

00:20:54.230 --> 00:20:58.280
gives me some options for
where I might want to publish.

00:20:58.280 --> 00:21:01.310
I'll say that I want to
publish to Quarto Pub.

00:21:01.310 --> 00:21:04.080
I have previously
linked my account here.

00:21:04.080 --> 00:21:06.300
So if this is your
first time doing it,

00:21:06.300 --> 00:21:09.050
it's going to ask you to
actually create and link

00:21:09.050 --> 00:21:09.890
an account.

00:21:09.890 --> 00:21:13.970
But yes, I do want to.

00:21:13.970 --> 00:21:16.670
And I do want to authorize this.

00:21:19.310 --> 00:21:21.620
The site name
comes from the name

00:21:21.620 --> 00:21:23.960
that I gave it in
my Quarto file,

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:28.670
so that was welcome to
Quarto, and has simply created

00:21:28.670 --> 00:21:30.950
a version of it without spaces.

00:21:30.950 --> 00:21:32.540
And I'm going to hit Enter.

00:21:32.540 --> 00:21:34.490
And we can see
that it's rendering

00:21:34.490 --> 00:21:36.450
my page one more time.

00:21:36.450 --> 00:21:39.890
And it is now shooting this--

00:21:39.890 --> 00:21:43.610
once it's rendered,
publishing it on Quarto Pub.

00:21:43.610 --> 00:21:45.180
All right, and here we go.

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:47.840
We can actually see
that my page has

00:21:47.840 --> 00:21:50.360
been published on Quarto Pub.

00:21:50.360 --> 00:21:53.750
This is the view for
me to manage the site.

00:21:53.750 --> 00:21:56.280
I can delete the
site, for example,

00:21:56.280 --> 00:21:59.820
or change its Slug if I want.

00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:01.100
But I can also--

00:22:01.100 --> 00:22:03.650
I'm also provided
a direct link to it

00:22:03.650 --> 00:22:05.220
that I can share with others.

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:08.210
So I have the simple
website that I

00:22:08.210 --> 00:22:11.420
have created with the two
pages now published on the web

00:22:11.420 --> 00:22:13.590
and ready to share with others.

00:22:13.590 --> 00:22:16.580
I hope you found this
introduction useful.

00:22:16.580 --> 00:22:19.580
And what I will do
at the end of this

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:24.650
is commit and push all of my
changes to a GitHub repository

00:22:24.650 --> 00:22:28.130
where you can simply pick
things up from where I left off

00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:32.050
and continue your
exploration of Quarto.

